<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tyras</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 375 CE (approx.) · capital Tyras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Greek colony at the mouth of the Dniester whose site, later the fortress of Akkerman, was inhabited for more than two thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded Tyras around 600 BCE on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> estuary, at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi</a>. The town took its name from the river, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Tyras, and is sometimes linked with an earlier settlement called Ophiusa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tyras was a small self-governing polis run by magistrates and a council on the usual Greek model. It lived by trading wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and fine pottery to the peoples of the hinterland in return for grain, fish, hides and slaves, and it minted its own coins. Its neighbours were in turn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> sacked the town around 50 BCE. It recovered under Roman protection from the 1st century CE, with a Roman garrison and links to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia-inferior">Moesia Inferior</a>. Gothic raids in the 3rd century and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> ended the ancient city, though the site was later rebuilt as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Moncastro</a> and Akkerman.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tyras.jpg" alt="Tyras" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Minami Himemiya · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyras4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 375 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tyras</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ophiusa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-112&amp;focus=tyras" aria-label="Tyras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5396 552 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tyras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5716 808L5552 825M5716 808L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5552 825h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5716 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5716 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-112&amp;focus=tyras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

